          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion that for certain tasks the power of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            potentially drastically surpasses that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"} computation
            (e.g. via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+algorithms"} quantum algorithms
            such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shor%27s+algorithm"} Shor's algorithm
            %em quantum supremacy
            (
            %a{:href => "#Preskill13"}> Preskill 2013
            , there related to the computational resource of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+entanglement"} quantum entanglement
            = succeed "." do
              %em quantum advantage
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+complexity+theory"} quantum complexity theory
          %h2#References References
          %h3#general General
          %p The term “quantum supremacy” is due to:
          %ul
            %li#Preskill13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Preskill"}> John Preskill
              \:
              = succeed ":" do
                %em Quantum computing and the entanglement frontier
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Theory of the Quantum World – Proceedings of the 25th Solvay Conference on Physics
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.5813"} arXiv:1203.5813
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/8674"} doi:10.1142/8674
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://simons.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/docs/394/preskilljohn.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Rønnow et al.
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Defining and detecting quantum speedup
                %strong 345
                6195 (2014) 420-424 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1252319"}> doi:10.1126/science.1252319
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_supremacy"} Quantum supremacy
          %p Review and outlook:
          %blockquote
            %p
              see also at
              %em
                %a{:href => "quantum+computation#NISQLimitations"} quantum computation – References - Limits of NISQ
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+Aaronson"}> Scott Aaronson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em How Much Structure Is Needed for Huge Quantum Speedups?
                %em 28th Solvay Physics Conference
                in Brussels on May 21, 2022 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.06930"}> arXiv:2209.06930
                , slides:
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.scottaaronson.com/talks/aar-solvay.ppt"} ppt
            %li
              %p
                Torsten Hoefler, Thomas Haener,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthias+Troyer"} Matthias Troyer
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Disentangling Hype from Practicality: On Realistically Achieving Quantum Advantage
                %strong 66
                5 (May 2023) 82-87 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00523"}> arXiv:2307.00523
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3571725"} acm pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/3571725"} doi:10.1145/3571725
              %blockquote
                %p “A large range of problem areas with quadratic quantum speedups, such as many current machine learning training approaches, accelerating drug design and protein folding with Grover’s algorithm, speeding up Monte Carlo simulations through quantum walks, as well as more traditional scientific computing simulations including the solution of many non-linear systems of equations, such as fluid dynamics in the turbulent regime, weather, and climate simulations will not achieve quantum advantage with current quantum algorithms in the foreseeable future.”
            %li
              %p
                Ryan Babbush, Jarrod R. McClean, Michael Newman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Craig+Gidney"} Craig Gidney
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Focus beyond Quadratic Speedups for Error-Corrected Quantum Advantage
                %strong 2
                010103 (2021) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PRXQuantum.2.010103"}> doi:10.1103/PRXQuantum.2.010103
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#LaRose24
              %p
                Ryan LaRose:
                %em A brief history of quantum vs classical computational advantage
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.14703"}> arXiv:2412.14703
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Emphasis of the need of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"} topological stabilization
          %ul
            %li#DasSarma22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sankar+Das+Sarma"}> Sankar Das Sarma
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum computing has a hype problem
                %a{:href => "https://www.technologyreview.com/2022/03/28/1048355/quantum-computing-has-a-hype-problem/"} MIT Technology Review (March 2022)
              %blockquote
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qubit"} qubit
                  systems we have today are a tremendous scientific achievement, but they take us no closer to having a quantum computer that can solve a problem that anybody cares about.
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi ⋯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\cdots]
                  What is missing is the breakthrough […] bypassing
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} quantum error correction
                  by using far-more-stable qubits, in an approach called
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computing"} topological quantum computing
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Takashi Yamakawa, Mark Zhandry,
              %em Verifiable Quantum Advantage without Structure
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.02063"}> arXiv:2204.02063
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#experimental_realizations Experimental realizations
          %p Claimed experimental demonstration of “quantum supremacy” (“quantum advantage”) in (very) special purpose applications:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                F. Arute et al.
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum supremacy using a programmable superconducting processor
                %strong 574
                (2019) 505–510 (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1666-5"}> doi:10.1038/s41586-019-1666-5
                )
            %li
              %p
                Han-Sen Zhong et al.,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum computational advantage using photons
                %strong 370
                6523 (2020) 1460-1463 (
                %a{:href => "https://science.sciencemag.org/content/370/6523/1460"}> doi:10.1126/science.abe8770
                )
          %p Critical analysis:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gil+Kalai"}> Gil Kalai
              , Yosef Rinott, Tomer Shoham,
              %em Google’s 2019 “Quantum Supremacy” Claims: Data, Documentation, and Discussion
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.12753"}> arXiv:2210.12753
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Classical computations competing with these claims:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Zhao et al.:
                %em Leapfrogging Sycamore: Harnessing 1432 GPUs for 7× Faster Quantum Random Circuit Sampling
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18889"}> arXiv:2406.18889
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  “Here we report an energy-efficient classical simulation algorithm, using 1432 GPUs &amp;lbrack;which is&amp;rbrack; 7 times faster than
                  %em Sycamore
                  53 qubits experiment.”
                %p arXiv Comments: “This work was completed on August 2023. A further 50x improvement has been achieved and will be posted on arXiv shortly”
          %p#ExperimentalReview Review and status reports:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Adrian Cho,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Google claims quantum computing milestone
                %strong 365
                6460 (2019) 1364 (
                %a{:href => "https://science.sciencemag.org/content/365/6460/1364"}> doi:10.1126/science.365.6460.1364
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Ball"}> Philip Ball
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Physicists in China challenge Google’s “quantum advantage”
                %strong 588
                380 (2020) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-020-03434-7"}> doi:10.1038/d41586-020-03434-7
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Barry C. Sanders,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "https://physics.aps.org/articles/v14/147"} Quantum Leap for Quantum Primacy
                %strong 14
                147 (2021)
          %p
            Quantum advantage for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grover%27s+algorithm"} Grover's algorithm
          %ul
            %li
              Bibek Pokharel, Daniel Lidar,
              %em Better-than-classical Grover search via quantum error detection and suppression
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.04543"}> arXiv:2211.04543
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
